r two more chows (T4-5-6 and B1-2-3). If they can form those, the hand would have four chows and a pair. Wait, but four chows would require 12 tiles, plus a pair. The user has 14 tiles. So if they have four chows (each 3 tiles) and a pair, that's 3*4 + 2 = 14. So yes. If they can form four chows and a pair, that's a Pure Straight (24 points) or Full Flush (24 points). Both are high-scoring.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard B9, they might be able to form a pair with another B9. But B9 is a terminal, so forming a pair would be part of a hand with All Terminals (8 points) if all melds are terminals. But the user also has non-terminal tiles.\n\nBut if they discard F2, the hand becomes all suited tiles, which could lead to Full Flush (24 points) if all melds are suited. So that's a high score.\n\nAnother consideration: the user's Seat Wind is South Wind. The Prevailing Wind is North. Since the user's hand is all suited tiles and no wind or dragon tiles, the Seat Wind and Prevailing Wind patterns (like Prevalent Wind or Seat Wind) are not applicable here. But if they have a pair of F2, they could get Seat Wind (2 points). But if they discard F2, they lose that possibility. However, the Full Flush is 24 points, which is much higher. So the trade-off is between 24 points for Full Flush vs 2 points for Seat Wind. Since the user needs at least 8 points to win, Full Flush is better.\n\nTherefore, discarding F2 seems optimal. It allows for the possibility of a Full Flush (24 points) if the user can form four chows and a pair. The required tiles to complete the chows are T6 and B3. Since T6 is available (T6:4 in unshown) and B3 is available (B3:4), the chances are good if the user can draw those.\n\nAlternatively, if they discard T5, they might be able to form a pair with another T5, but T5 is a middle tile. Not as valuable as forming a Full Flush.\n\nSo the best move is to discard F2, allowing the hand to be all suited tiles and aiming for a Full Flush (24 points) or other high-scoring patterns.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discarding F2 allows the hand to remain all suited tiles, potentially forming a Full Flush (24 points) with four chows and a pair. This is a high-scoring pattern compared to other options.\nReason: The hand currently has two chows (W4-5-6 and B4-5-6) and potential for two more chows (T4-5-6 and B1-2-3). Discarding F2 eliminates the only honor tile, focusing on all-suited tiles for Full Flush. T6 and B3 are available in the wall to complete the chows. Other options risk breaking existing chows or reducing scoring potential.\nAnswer: Play F2"}
